WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.770
Before getting into the best of this episode
in which Joel, Tess, and Ellie have to maneuver

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.630
through a Museum of clickers, I want to acknowledge
the cold open again.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:12.790
I find it really interesting the direction
that Craig Mazin is going for the start of

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:16.930
these episodes because it feels like the book
for World War Z so far.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.810
This is also assuming that this continues
to be the case for the following episodes,

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:24.580
but in the book World War Z, it was essentially
a collection of different points of views

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.130
throughout the world during a zombie outbreak.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:31.370
It was a great read because it offered a lot
of variety and substance because every perspective

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.340
the book explored was unique.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.110
So the last of us so far is kind of going
down this route because we have two new and

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.390
original perspectives that further expand
upon the world.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:44.090
For this one, it was intriguing to see the
initial start of the outbreak and to have

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:49.090
someone explain with expertise and fully educate
the audience as to how someone gets infected

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.080
and for it to be more believable.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.780
Which makes this scenario even more realistic
and thus scary.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.359
Craig Mazin again is great at hitting you
with this gut-punch feeling of dread in which

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:04.080
the scientist acknowledges that this is an
extinction-level event and advises the military

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.760
officer to bomb the city.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.439
Truly a crushing form of advice that the scientist
gives, but she knows it’s necessary to save

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:11.439
the world.

00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:15.620
And this even has a callback in the episode
where Joel, Ellie, and Tess come across a

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:16.620
crater.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.020
For this cold open, it obviously isn’t in
the game because it would destroy any sort

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:24.680
of momentum in the gameplay if you cut to
some random scientist halfway across the world.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.710
However, for a show, you don’t have to constantly
keep the player engaged with action.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.009
For the point where we're at in the show,
there were about 7 action setpieces in the

00:01:34.009 --> 00:01:35.189
game thus far.

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:39.729
So I saw some people complaining about there
not being enough action and the show was boring

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:44.689
in comparison, but do you realize how much
that would ruin the tense nature of the show.

00:01:44.689 --> 00:01:48.939
For action to be satisfying, there has to
be stakes for it, build up, and by having

00:01:48.939 --> 00:01:53.130
too much of it, you lose out on character
development opportunities.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.950
Especially in the show that is more of a tense
thriller survival.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:01.579
If you are constantly having Joel kill dozens
of infected in every other scene then the

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:05.770
factor of danger starts to diminish because
the show would now be establishing the main

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.050
character as an action hero.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:12.030
For the game, you kind of are that, but you
have to keep in mind how many times you died

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.319
while playing the game assuming what difficulty
you played on.

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:19.680
Because I hate to break it to some people,
but you can’t respawn in a TV Show.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.910
Along with the fact that Joel had an extremely
hard time killing one clicker.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:28.349
So the mechanics in which you keep the player/viewer
scared for the well-being of the character

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:29.690
work differently.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:33.709
One is grounded in realism that we are forced
to watch and be on the edge of our seat, while

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:37.950
the other one is more of a challenging experience
and punishes you if you die.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:42.450
The show not having hours worth of action
is not a bad thing and if anything is a great

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.730
benefit that the show takes advantage of.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.489
Any of that lost time in terms of the game’s
length is converted into original scenes.

00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:54.300
Craig Mazin is able to simultaneously turn
the reduction of action into a double positive

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.290
because it frees up time for new material
and it gives the show a better pace and allows

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.310
the infected to be more intimidating.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.379
Because we aren’t constantly mowing them
down and we can use them in more of a horror

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:06.379
angle.

00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:10.080
Also through the show, Joel is able to have
a deeper impact through him killing a FEDRA

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:11.180
officer.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.810
In the game, you essentially kill dozens of
them and as the player, you become desensitized

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:23.950
to that violence.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:27.549
The show is able to make Joel even more human
because he’s dealing with the emotional

00:03:27.549 --> 00:03:30.310
consequences of taking one human life.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:35.130
And to also do this, you need that good pace
within the script so that we can have character-focused

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.190
scenes after big events.

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:41.520
As an example, this scene where Joel and Tess
are observing to see if Ellie turns wasn’t

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.530
in the game.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:46.530
It creates this balanced flow so that the
characters can properly process and react

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:48.390
to what recently happened.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.439
And again this is a great original scene.

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:54.030
It’s filled with good character-focused
conflict because they don’t buy the fact

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:58.020
that she’s actually immune and it offers
Ellie to try to disarm their fears with a

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:05.740
funny moment.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:09.720
Then before going into the terrifying scene
with the clickers I want to gush about how

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.629
beautiful the production design and set decoration
is in this show.

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:18.250
I was really excited to see how it was going
into this episode, but it thoroughly exceeded

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:23.310
my expectations with how insanely detailed
and worn down every square inch of this world

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:24.350
is.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:30.580
From vines and vegetation covering almost
every surface, flooded areas, animals roaming,

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:35.690
dilapidated structures, fungus sprawling from
decomposing corpses, gorgeous vistas that

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:41.370
are aided by seamless and abundant VFX, and
a frog that knows how to play the piano.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.650
This show is completely astounding as to how
detailed the world is and I’m dying to know

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:49.340
how much this aspect alone costed HBO in terms
of the budget.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.250
Because off of looking it up, each episode
has a budget of somewhere in the range of

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:56.590
10 to 15 million and it is really paying off.

00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:01.330
With that magnificent aspect of the show out
of the way, let’s finally get into the clicker

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:02.330
scene.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:06.031
To say that this scene was just scary or tense
would be an understatement because there are

00:05:06.031 --> 00:05:10.440
so many aspects that make this a perfect horror
and action scene.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.120
From the prior set-up of our characters being
like oh shit there’s a variant in this building.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.850
The false sense of success that our characters
gain when they navigated through this mad

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:22.090
house until the unlucky collapse of a roof
near them.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:26.760
The really slow build-up of the clickers making
their way to our protagonists and us hearing

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:27.760
them.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.070
Not giving the audience a clear view of the
clickers to make us even more terrified of

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:32.070
the unknown.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:36.000
The genius set location where there are glass
cases in which you can see clickers pass by

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.120
on the other side.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.180
The atmosphere of the room being dark and
foggy with the flashlights barely making out

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.800
the moving subjects.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.449
Our characters being absolutely scared to
the bone that they are about to die.

00:05:46.449 --> 00:05:50.790
And the final release of tension into chaotic
action with the close-up of the clicker screaming

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:52.410
was amazing.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:57.470
Every single aspect within this scene is perfect
and is all intelligently designed to elevate

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:58.470
the horror.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:02.560
I also love how the characters didn’t even
knock anything over to alert the clickers

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.330
and was purely just from Ellie breathing too
hard.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.970
It makes them that much more terrifying when
they can hear breathing because it’s almost

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.000
impossible to suppress that when your heart
is racing from fear.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.650
Then the action segment was just as good as
the horror build-up because of how hard these

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.090
clickers were to overcome.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:19.900
That’s one issue within the game because
you have an ability in which you miraculously

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:24.470
get echo-location in which you can see every
zombie through walls.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:28.310
This essentially destroys any scary aspect
within the game when you are trying to stealth

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:30.960
because it’s much easier when you have wall
hacks.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.940
However, in the show, Joel doesn’t have
superman X-ray vision and they have to pray

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.680
to god that they don’t get found by the
clickers.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:42.849
Another thing that you may not realize is
that reloading your gun can make noise.

00:06:42.849 --> 00:06:47.000
One of my favorite moments in this scene was
Joel slowly trying to reload and minimizing

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.620
his noise while a clicker slowly approaches
him.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.130
The cinematography of this moment aids the
suspense because the camera moves to a close-up

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:58.479
on Joel as the frame leaves out the clicker
and we don’t know how close it’s getting.

00:06:58.479 --> 00:07:03.340
We essentially enter Joel’s frightened perspective
and then move with him as we reveal the clicker

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:04.340
again.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:08.129
And even the pesky glass from the game that
you can step on makes an appearance and quickly

00:07:08.129 --> 00:07:10.479
turns the scene into a helpless struggle.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:15.220
If the rest of the show is able to consistently
execute scenes of this caliber then I can

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:17.419
honestly find no faults.

00:07:17.419 --> 00:07:22.530
Like this is just unmatched quality that has
every aspect of production firing on all cylinders

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:26.259
to deliver one of the best visual products
on the market right now.

00:07:26.259 --> 00:07:30.110
Then obviously the narrative continues to
hit some great beats in which Tess actually

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:33.060
got bit and is forced to stay behind and kill
the infected.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:38.150
An interesting change here is that Craig Mazin
made it to where the fungus is a single organism

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:41.440
that can be alerted miles away if you step
on its tendrils.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.370
It Makes for a great mechanic to further increase
the suspense because the characters are screwed

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.180
if they happen to step on it.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:51.670
Even Neil Druckman admitted that this was
a change that he loved.

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:55.940
Episode 2 of The Last of Us continues the
outstanding opening of the season and manages

00:07:55.940 --> 00:08:00.860
to create one of the most scary and intense
scenes in a zombie movie or show in a long

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:01.860
time.

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:05.750
And to think that the show is already seeming
like it's peaking now is a crazy thing because

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.530
there are still so many great sequences to
come.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:13.349
This episode proves that the first episode’s
quality wasn’t a fluke and again the execution

00:08:13.349 --> 00:08:14.840
is practically perfect.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:18.749
Thank you for watching and let me know what
you thought about this episode in the comments.

